drawAtmosphere:
```js
function drawAtmosphere(){
  let grad=ctx.createLinearGradient(0,0,0,H*.72);
  grad.addColorStop(0,'#2a2233');
  grad.addColorStop(.35,'#5b3340');
  grad.addColorStop(.62,'#a7523a');
  grad.addColorStop(.78,'#d97a43');
  grad.addColorStop(1,'#5d3a2c');
  ctx.fillStyle=grad;ctx.fillRect(0,0,W,H);
  // sun
  let s=project(-30,24,-180);
  let sunX = clamp(s.x, -W*.2, W*1.2), sunY = clamp(s.y, -H*.2, H*.75);
  let R=Math.min(W,H)*(.55);
  let g=ctx.createRadialGradient(sunX,sunY,2,sunX,sunY,R);
  g.addColorStop(0,'rgba(255,226,160,.95)');
  g.addColorStop(.08,'rgba(255,166,86,.65)');
  g.addColorStop(.35,'rgba(214,95,55,.22)');
  g.addColorStop(1,'rgba(120,50,50,0)');
  ctx.fillStyle=g;ctx.fillRect(0,0,W,H);
  // sun disc if in view
  if(s.d>0 && sunX>-50 && sunX<W+50 && sunY>-50 && sunY<H*.7){ctx.fillStyle='rgba(255,238,190,.9)';ctx.beginPath();ctx.arc(sunX,sunY,Math.max(10,Math.min(W,H)*.032),0,Math.PI*2);ctx.fill();}
  // haze band at horizon
  let hz=ctx.createLinearGradient(0,H*.32,0,H*.62);
  hz.addColorStop(0,'rgba(255,145,85,0)');
  hz.addColorStop(.6,'rgba(255,150,95,.12)');
  hz.addColorStop(1,'rgba(255,120,70,0)');
  ctx.fillStyle=hz;ctx.fillRect(0,H*.32,W,H*.3);
}
```
Wait sun y: project y = H*.43 - up*focal/d; up = 24-2.65=21.35 plus pitch effects. d≈? forward with pitch: dx etc. ~180; focal≈.92*720=662; 21.35*662/180≈78 above H*.43 → y≈232 for H=720. That's within horizon band. Good.

But top sky visibility: In this renderer, the world has no sky occlusion — walls are drawn over background. Interior ceiling absent, so sky visible above walls. That's okay: sunset sky behind house silhouette. But indoors, the sky gradient shows through above interior walls making it feel open. Acceptable stylistically (roofless cutaway). Could add a subtle "ceiling" dark band? The original had dark; now sunset sky will be visible indoors too. It's fine—cutaway view.

